<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariège (department)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1790</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariège is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, created in 1790 from the County of Foix and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a>, with its prefecture at Foix. Named after the river Ariège, it is mountainous and thinly populated, and its castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> are associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariege-department.jpg" alt="Ariège (department)" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olybrius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont%C3%A9gut-en-Couserans_-_Ussau_-_20110702_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
